9: The strongest defense is documented. The [v1.1 amendment](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-PHYSICS-SUCCESSOR-PRIORITY-DECLARATION-v1.1-AMENDMENT-2026-09-08.md:6) calls 50 Mbit/s a *nominal*, synthetic operating point, expressly permits partial delivery, credits achieved full-buffer ACM bits and separates PHY service from target attainment. The [original declaration](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-PHYSICS-SUCCESSOR-PRIORITY-DECLARATION-2026-09-08.md:14) declares no guaranteed-rate endpoint. Most decisively, [v1.9 §1](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-PHYSICS-SUCCESSOR-PRIORITY-DECLARATION-v1.9-AMENDMENT-2026-09-09.md:5) explicitly requires: **“The margin must not be re-solved into transmit power.”** It fixes nominal powers, applies the quantile only to wanted-link predicted reception, and objects specifically to increased interference and changed cap feasibility. The proposed change reverses that instruction.
10: 
11: The coherent convention is to spend power sufficient for a nominal operating point, then choose a more robust, usually slower mode under a conservative channel view. For that defense to hold, the nominal target must be a control setpoint rather than promised delivery; the lower selected mode must actually be transmitted; failed attempts must still incur energy and interference; partial delivery and conservative rejection must be permitted; and neither guaranteed 50 Mbit/s nor 90% network availability may be claimed. Those conditions substantially match the declarations and implementation. [v1.7 §1](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-PHYSICS-SUCCESSOR-PRIORITY-DECLARATION-v1.7-ERRATUM-2026-09-09.md:5) had already withdrawn the availability rationale and named a prespecified tenth-percentile scoring convention.
